  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166863,
      "id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e4c13360-b84e-54f6-ba97-bbcbe1e9d775",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(- 8 x^{2} - 4 y)\\,i+(4 x^{2} + 2 y)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(- 2 y e^{x} + 2)\\,i+(16 y - 2 e^{x})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(2 y)\\,i+(2 x)\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=- 8 x^{2} - 4 y$ and $Q=4 x^{2} + 2 y$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=-4,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=8 x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=- 2 y e^{x} + 2$ and $Q=16 y - 2 e^{x}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 e^{x},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 2 e^{x}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2 y$ and $Q=2 x$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b66e65c6-523e-510f-bb7f-3b498d744f8c",
