  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166863,
      "id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9355504c-a53a-51fc-b5a9-a49349a04fbd",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(- x y)\\,i+(- \\frac{x^{2}}{2} - y^{2})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(4 x + y^{2})\\,i+(4 x y^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(\\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)})\\,i+(2 \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)})\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=- x y$ and $Q=- \\frac{x^{2}}{2} - y^{2}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4 x + y^{2}$ and $Q=4 x y^{2}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4 y^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)}$ and $Q=2 \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 4 y \\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
